  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late \nWednesday evening reveal a highly amplified synoptic pattern is in \nplace atop the CONUS, with ridging extending from the southern \nplains northward through south-central Canada, and a powerful cut-\noff low just off the west coast. At the surface, broad high pressure \noverspreads nearly the entire CONUS east of the Rockies, supporting \nlight and variable winds across southwest KS. Through the remainder \nof the overnight period, light winds and clear skies will aid in \nThursday morning lows dropping into the upper 40s to low 50s. \nDaytime Thursday, short range guidance indicates the aforementioned \nsynoptic features will slowly shift eastward, with the core of the \nupper trough approaching the Intermountain West by 00Z Friday. In \nresponse, lee cyclogenesis will begin in eastern MT and adjacent \nareas, which will tighten the pressure gradient across the central \nplains to result in southerly winds increasing into the 15-25 mph \nrange gusting to 35 mph. Additionally, 850-mb temperatures will warm \nby 2-4 degrees C, which will translate to afternoon highs rising \nwell above normal into the upper 70s to low 80s. Overnight Thursday, \nsoutherly winds will remain elevated, fostering weaker radiational \ncooling and Friday morning lows in the upper 50s.\n\nDaytime Friday, short range ensembles agree the deep upper wave will \ncontinue to advance eastward towards the central Rockies. As DPVA \nincreases over the High Plains, the lee cyclone will deepen to sub-\n1000-mb, engendering strong southerly winds across southwest KS. At \ntheir peak during the late morning through mid-afternoon, winds will \nbe sustained in the 20-30 mph range gusting to 40-45 mph. On the \nother hand, temperatures will begin to cool as decreasing 500-mb \nheights brings afternoon highs down into the mid to upper 70s.\n\nSaturday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nsuggest the strong upper trough will stall out over the central \nRockies during the weekend before another shortwave trough diving \nsoutheast from south-central Canada finally kicks it out onto the \nHigh Plains early next business week. This synoptic evolution is \nideal for precipitation chances across our area, with rainfall \nexpected to begin across our western zones Saturday, become more \nwidespread daytime Sunday, then exiting stage right on Monday. Once \nall is said and done early Tuesday, both the ECMWF ENS and GEFS mean \ntotal precipitation fields show 0.5-1\" of rainfall roughly along and \neast of a Hays-Meade line, and 1-2\" to the west. Temperatures will \nalso fall considerably during this time frame, with afternoon highs \nin the mid 70s on Saturday dropping into the upper 50s/low 60s \nMonday and Tuesday.",
